  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "The volume of a cube increases at a rate that's proportional to the cube of its side length. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cube **increases** at a rate that's **proportional** to its **side length**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cube* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *proportional to the cube of the side length.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cube is given by\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =k{s}^{3} \\\\ 3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =k{s}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k{s}^{3}}{3{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{ks}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{3}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=Ks.\n$$",
